# Agricultural Activities Tracker

A simple, farmer-focused activity tracker to plan and monitor agricultural
tasks and operations — sowing, weeding, irrigation, harvesting, and other
field activities. Create activities, track status (Not Started / In Progress /
Completed), set priority, search and filter, and deploy easily to common
Node hosting platforms.

## Technologies used

**Frontend**
- React 19 (Vite, plain JavaScript — no TypeScript, no UI framework)
- Custom CSS design system (CSS variables), no Tailwind/Bootstrap
- Plain `fetch` for API calls — no Axios, no state-management library
  (component state + one small Context for toast notifications is enough
  for this app's size)

**Backend**
- Node.js + Express — REST API
- `better-sqlite3` — synchronous SQLite driver
- `cors` — cross-origin access, configurable for split deployments

**Database**
- SQLite (single file, `backend/tasks.db`), schema created automatically on
  first run — no separate database server to install or manage

No build tooling beyond Vite, no ORM, no external services required to run
this locally.

## Project structure

```
task-manager/
├── backend/
│   ├── db.js              # SQLite connection + schema (creates tasks table)
│   ├── server.js          # Express app: middleware, static frontend, routes
│   ├── routes/
│   │   └── tasks.js       # All /tasks REST endpoints + validation
│   └── package.json
└── frontend/
    ├── index.html
    ├── vite.config.js     # dev proxy to the backend on :4000
    ├── package.json
    ├── public/
    │   └── leaf.svg
    └── src/
        ├── main.jsx           # React root, wraps App in ToastProvider
        ├── App.jsx            # Top-level state, data fetching, shortcuts
        ├── api.js             # fetch wrapper for the REST API
        ├── utils.js           # relative-time + greeting helpers
        ├── index.css          # design tokens (color, type, spacing)
        ├── app.css            # component styles
        ├── hooks/
        │   ├── useTheme.js    # day/night mode, persisted to localStorage
        │   └── useToast.jsx   # toast notification context
        └── components/
            ├── TopBar.jsx
            ├── GrowthPanel.jsx   # stats + animated progress ring
            ├── TaskForm.jsx      # create/edit form
            ├── FilterBar.jsx     # status chips + search
            ├── TaskList.jsx
            ├── TaskCard.jsx
            └── EmptyState.jsx
```

## How to run it locally

Requires Node.js 18+ (tested on Node 22).

**1. Start the backend** (in one terminal):
```bash
cd backend
npm install
npm start
```
This starts the API on `http://localhost:4000` and creates `tasks.db`
automatically.

**2. Start the frontend** (in a second terminal):
```bash
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev
```
This starts the Vite dev server (usually `http://localhost:5173`) and
proxies any `/tasks` or `/health` request to the backend on port 4000, so
open **http://localhost:5173** and it just works — no extra config.

### Running as a single process (production-style)

Build the frontend, then let the Express server serve it:
```bash
cd frontend && npm install && npm run build
cd ../backend && npm install && npm start
```
Open **http://localhost:4000** — Express serves the built React app and the
API from the same origin. This is exactly how it runs in the single-service
deployment described below.

## How the database is set up

No manual setup needed. On startup, `backend/db.js`:
1. Opens (or creates) `backend/tasks.db`, a local SQLite file.
2. Runs `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS tasks (...)` to ensure the schema exists.

**`tasks` table:**

| Column      | Type    | Notes                                        |
|-------------|---------|-----------------------------------------------|
| id          | INTEGER | Primary key, auto-increment                  |
| title       | TEXT    | Required (activity name)                      |
| description | TEXT    | Optional, details about the activity         |
| status      | TEXT    | `Not Started`, `Pending` (In Progress), `Completed` |
| priority    | TEXT    | `Low`, `Medium`, or `High` (CHECK constraint) |
| createdAt   | TEXT    | ISO 8601 timestamp, set server-side           |

To reset the data, stop the server and delete `backend/tasks.db` (plus the
`-wal`/`-shm` files if present) — it's recreated empty on next start.

To point at a different SQLite file location (useful in deployment, see
below), set `DB_PATH`:
```bash
DB_PATH=/data/tasks.db npm start
```

## API reference

Base URL: `http://localhost:4000` (or wherever the backend is deployed)

| Method | Endpoint            | Purpose                                                        |
|--------|----------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|
| GET    | `/tasks`             | List tasks. Supports `?status=`, `?priority=`, `?search=`       |
| GET    | `/tasks/:id`         | Get a single task                                                |
| POST   | `/tasks`              | Create a task                                                    |
| PUT    | `/tasks/:id`          | Update a task (full or partial body)                             |
| PATCH  | `/tasks/:id/status`   | Convenience endpoint to just set status                          |
| DELETE | `/tasks/:id`          | Delete a task                                                    |
| GET    | `/health`             | Health check                                                     |

**Create/update body shape:**
```json
{
  "title": "Water the tomatoes",
  "description": "Morning routine",
  "status": "Pending",
  "priority": "High"
}
```
`title` is the only required field on create. `status` defaults to
`Pending` and `priority` defaults to `Medium` if omitted. Invalid `status`
or `priority` values return `400` with a descriptive error.

## Frontend features

- Create, edit, delete, and toggle activities, with optimistic status updates
- Filter by status (All / Pending / Completed) and live search, both backed
  by real `?status=`/`?search=` query params on the API — not client-side
  filtering of a full list
- A "growth ring" stats panel — tasks planted / growing / harvested, with
  an animated completion percentage
- Day/night theme toggle, remembered across visits
- Toast notifications for create/update/delete instead of browser alerts
- Inline two-step delete confirmation (no `window.confirm` popup)
- Keyboard shortcuts: `n` focuses the new-task field, `/` focuses search,
  `Esc` cancels an in-progress edit
- Server-side field validation surfaced inline in the form, not just as a
  failed request
- Responsive down to mobile, visible keyboard focus states, and
  `prefers-reduced-motion` respected

## Technical decisions

- **better-sqlite3 over an async driver**: synchronous calls keep the route
  handlers simple, and SQLite means zero external services — the backend
  runs from `npm install && npm start` with nothing else to provision.
- **Plain React + Vite, no TypeScript, no state library**: the app's state
  is a handful of `useState` calls in `App.jsx`; introducing Redux/Zustand
  or TypeScript would add ceremony without solving a real problem at this
  size, and it's easier to explain and defend a smaller surface area.
- **One server for API + frontend in the default deployment path**: avoids
  CORS/proxy configuration in production — everything is reachable from one
  origin. `cors` is still configurable via `FRONTEND_ORIGIN` for the split
  deployment option below.
- **Validation lives in the route layer** (`routes/tasks.js`): required
  fields and enum checks (`status`, `priority`) are enforced server-side
  independent of what the client sends, returning `400` with field-level
  errors.
- **Separate `PATCH /tasks/:id/status`**: the spec's "mark as Pending or
  Completed" action is a single field, so a small dedicated endpoint keeps
  the common case a single clear API call, while `PUT /tasks/:id` still
  supports editing everything at once.
- **Optimistic UI on status toggling**: the checkbox updates instantly and
  rolls back with a toast if the request fails, so the app feels responsive
  on a real network.

## Deploying the application

The app tracks agricultural activities and consists of a React frontend and
an Express backend that uses a single SQLite file. You can deploy as a
single service (backend serves frontend) or split frontend and backend.

This repository includes example GitHub Actions workflows that automate
deploys on push: a frontend deploy to Vercel and a backend deploy trigger
for Render. To use them you must add repository secrets (see below).

### Option A — single service (recommended, matches this repo's default setup)

Any platform that runs a persistent Node process with an attached disk
works: **Render**, **Railway**, or **Fly.io**. Steps below use Render as the
concrete example.

1. Push this repository to GitHub.
2. In Render, create a **New Web Service** from the repo.
3. **Build command:**
   ```
   cd frontend && npm install && npm run build && cd ../backend && npm install
   ```
4. **Start command:**
   ```
   cd backend && npm start
   ```
5. **Environment variables:**
   - `PORT` — Render sets this automatically; the app already reads
     `process.env.PORT`.
   - `DB_PATH` — set to a path on a persistent disk, e.g. `/data/tasks.db`
     (see next step).
6. **Add a persistent disk** (Render → your service → Disks) mounted at
   `/data`. Without this, `tasks.db` lives on the container's ephemeral
   filesystem and **is wiped on every redeploy** — the disk is what makes
   the data durable.
7. Deploy. Render gives you a URL like `https://sprout-tasks.onrender.com`
   serving both the UI and the API.

The same recipe (build the frontend, start the backend, attach a volume for
`tasks.db`) works on Railway (Volumes) or Fly.io (`fly volumes create`).

### Option B — frontend and backend deployed separately

Use this if you want the frontend on a CDN-backed static host (Vercel,
Netlify) independent from the API.

**Backend** (Render, Railway, or Fly.io — a persistent Node service, same
disk caveat as above):
1. Deploy the `backend/` folder as its own service (root directory:
   `backend`, build command: `npm install`, start command: `npm start`).
2. Set `DB_PATH` to a persistent-disk path as in Option A.
3. Set `FRONTEND_ORIGIN` to your deployed frontend's URL, e.g.
   `https://sprout-tasks.vercel.app` — this restricts the API's CORS policy
   to that origin instead of allowing all origins.
4. Note the backend's public URL, e.g. `https://sprout-api.onrender.com`.

**Frontend** (Vercel or Netlify):
1. Import the repo, set the project root to `frontend/`.
2. Build command: `npm run build`. Output directory: `dist`.
3. Add an environment variable `VITE_API_URL` set to your backend's URL
   from above (e.g. `https://sprout-api.onrender.com`) — `src/api.js`
   already reads this at build time and prefixes every API call with it.
4. Deploy. Vercel/Netlify gives you a URL like
   `https://sprout-tasks.vercel.app` for the UI, talking to the backend
   over the internet.

### A note on SQLite in production

SQLite is a single file on disk, which is exactly why the deployment steps
above call out a **persistent disk/volume** — most container platforms
otherwise reset the filesystem on every deploy or restart, silently wiping
your tasks. This is a fine, honest trade-off for an app this size (and easy
to defend: "it's a file, so it needs a volume, unlike a managed Postgres
instance"); if the app needed to scale across multiple server instances,
that would be the point to migrate to a hosted Postgres/MySQL database
instead — the `better-sqlite3` calls are isolated to `db.js` and
`routes/tasks.js`, so that migration wouldn't touch the frontend at all.

## Not included

User authentication, pagination, and automated tests were left out to keep
the submission focused; the codebase is small enough (one Express router,
a handful of React components) that any of these could be added
incrementally without restructuring.

## Live demo

Not deployed by default — follow "Deploying the application" above to put
it online, then add the live URL here.

### CI / Deploy secrets (GitHub Actions)

If you use the included GitHub Actions workflows to deploy on push, add these
secrets to the repository (Settings → Secrets → Actions):

- `VERCEL_TOKEN` — Vercel personal access token
- `VERCEL_ORG_ID` — Vercel organization id
- `VERCEL_PROJECT_ID` — Vercel project id for the frontend
- `RENDER_API_KEY` — Render API key to trigger backend deploys
- `RENDER_SERVICE_ID` — Render service id for your backend

If you prefer different hosts (Netlify, Railway), the repo can be wired to
those providers instead; tell me which provider and I will add or update
the workflow accordingly.
